      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the slopes of both lines.\n2. Use the relationship that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\).\n3. Solve for \\(a\\).\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. **Identify the slopes of both lines:**\n\n   The first equation is already written in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\):\n\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + 13\n   \\]\n\n   Here, the slope \\(m_1\\) is \\(2\\).\n\n   For the second equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\), we need to rewrite it in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\):\n\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6y = -ax + 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\]\n\n   Here, the slope \\(m_2\\) is \\(-\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\n2. **Use the perpendicular line condition:**\n\n   Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\):\n\n   \\[\n   m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the slopes we identified:\n\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(a\\):**\n\n   Simplify the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot -\\frac{a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\n   \\[\n   a = 3\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this calculation using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variable\na = symbols('a')\n\n# Slope of the first line\nm1 = 2\n\n# Slope of the second line\nm2 = -a / 6\n\n# Perpendicular slope condition\nperpendicular_condition = Eq(m1 * m2, -1)\n\n# Solve for a\nsolution = solve(perpendicular_condition, a)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
