      "text": "To determine if two lines are perpendicular, their slopes must satisfy a specific relationship. Specifically, if the slope of the first line is \\( m_1 \\) and the slope of the second line is \\( m_2 \\), the lines are perpendicular if and only if \\( m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\).\n\nThe first line is given by the equation \\( y = 2x + 13 \\). From this, we can see that the slope \\( m_1 \\) of the first line is 2.\n\nNext, we need to rewrite the second line equation to find its slope. The second line equation is \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\). We first isolate \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\( m_2 \\) of the second line is therefore \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nTo check for perpendicularity, we set up the equation \\( m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by -3:\n\n\\[\n(a) = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we solve this by directly substituting:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{3}{6} \\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies correctly to:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( \\boxed{3} \\) is the correct value, confirming our solution.\n\\(\\boxed{3}\\)",
